Fix: linux-api-headers >= 6.x renamed the "am I being assembled?" guard from
__ASSEMBLY__ to __ASSEMBLER__. arm_nce_asm_definitions.h still defines
__ASSEMBLY__, so asm-generic/signal.h no longer takes its assembly branch and
typedefs its own sigset_t. glibc's sigset_t (bits/types/sigset_t.h, pulled in
by <random>/<stdlib.h> before this header in e.g. k_process.cpp) is already
declared -> "conflicting declaration 'typedef struct sigset_t sigset_t'".

Defining the modern guard as well restores the original intent of the header
(it only ships SIGxxx/NRxxx constants and offsets; every UAPI struct it used
to leak is provided by glibc in C++ TUs). struct sigcontext is untouched: it
lives in <asm/sigcontext.h>, a different header.

--- a/src/core/arm/nce/arm_nce_asm_definitions.h
+++ b/src/core/arm/nce/arm_nce_asm_definitions.h
@@ -3,7 +3,13 @@
 
 #pragma once
 
 #define __ASSEMBLY__
+
+/* linux-api-headers >= 6.x: the guard is __ASSEMBLER__ now. Without this the
+   UAPI sigset_t/sigaction below collide with glibc's in every C++ TU. */
+#ifndef __ASSEMBLER__
+#define __ASSEMBLER__ 1
+#endif
 
 #include <asm-generic/signal.h>
 #include <asm-generic/unistd.h>
